Based on github.com/coder/websocket v1.8.15, retaining its MIT license.

Campfire additions (all under ENGINE-40, behind the hub's
`CAMPFIRE_CABLE_FAST` switch for the hub-level paths):

- prepared.go: PreparedMessage shares immutable payloads and lazily
  compressed bytes across server connections negotiating permessage-deflate
  without context takeover. `WritePreparedBatch` coalesces up to
  `BatchMaxFrames` (64) prepared frames into one vectored network write
  (writev on TCP conns) with reused per-connection scratch; other connection
  modes fall back to the ordinary writer.
- Header-first server reads: `accept.go` discards the hijacked 4KiB read
  buffer (replaying only the bytes net/http already buffered), so a freshly
  accepted socket holds no read buffer at all. The first frame read lazily
  allocates a 512-byte scratch (`read.go` `readScratchSize`), and payload
  reads at or above that size bypass the scratch with one exact-length
  syscall (bufio passes them straight through). `Conn.Read` reads a single
  message into one exact-length buffer; fragmented and compressed messages
  hand off to the unchanged streaming reader with identical wire behavior.
- Frame-level bytes are identical to v1.8.15 for every case (verified by
  byte-for-byte batch-vs-sequential tests and the whole upstream suite).

Behavioral notes:
- A 30s write deadline now bounds a whole batch (one wake) rather than each
  frame; the hub's slow-client 256-frame queue and overflow disconnect are
  unchanged.
- Client connections and tests keep the upstream buffered path untouched.